+# FAA liaison log, same-day NORAD review and FAA event-tracker leads
+
+Reviewed November 9, 2026 Eastern. B129 / PD01 / PD07. A National Archives release preserves Commission interview notes about a liaison's contemporaneous log and a same-day review at the Cheyenne Mountain Operations Center (CMOC). Separate Team 7 notes identify paper inputs to FAA event tracking. They supply distinct routes to the underlying records.
+
+## Record and custody observations
+
+| Record | Observation |
+| --- | --- |
+| ISCAP 2011-048 document 39, pp. 1 and 5–6 | The opening handwritten page dates the Reggie Settles interview **July 8, 2004**. P. 5 records uncertainty about who first told him about United 93 and mentions the FAA Command Center and NEADS as sources of situational information. P. 6 records that he kept a log and **“he thinks it got thrown away.”** This is an attributed recollection. The original log and a disposition record are unacquired. |
+| Same item, p. 6 middle and p. 9 item 11 | P. 6 records participation in a CMOC review on **9/11/01**, led by a person written **Col. Hoddinott**, and says information from **H.W. notes** was made available. P. 9 separately asks about participation in a **Capt. Hoddinott** group meeting concerning the chronology. Keep the rank labels, the wording **H.W. notes** and the question form. The source leaves the identity of the notes, the relationship of the meetings and the form of any handoff open. |
+| Team 7 Box 21 McLaughlin interview notes, pp. 4–5 | P. 4 refers to an **“event tracker”** and says event recorders were fed hard-copy data from the primary and tactical nets. P. 5 records a recollection of separate personal notes that could not be found. These are leads to the FAA event-tracker inputs and outputs. The wording gives no audio capture, item number or archive receipt. |
+| Same Team 7 file, p. 9 opening | The notes describe routine FAA hotwash, timelines and after-action activity and add **“that wasn't done on 9/11.”** Preserve this account of an FAA process alongside the account of a CMOC meeting. The two sources describe different settings. The wording does not establish the exact scope of the FAA statement, the existence of a CMOC report or the absence of every FAA review. |
+
+[NARA ISCAP release index](https://www.archives.gov/declassification/iscap/pdf/2011-048.html) · [Official Settles interview notes, pp. 5–6](https://www.archives.gov/files/declassification/iscap/pdf/2011-048-doc39.pdf#page=6) · [Public Commission ZIP containing the Team 7 McLaughlin notes](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip)
+
+## Keep the United 93 time labels separate
+
+- **Before 10:03** appears twice in the handwritten staff list on Settles p. 8, items 1–2, about the reported absence of references in chat and handwritten operations logs. Items 4–5 refer to interviews that the staff considered when evaluating the liaison account. The original logs and their completeness were outside this unit.
+- **10:07** appears in item 3 on p. 8 for a question from NMCC to NORAD about information on United 93. The 2006 DOT OIG report, p. 4 lines 17–29, separately records the Commission's **10:07 a.m.** finding for FAA notification to NEADS that the flight had been hijacked.
+- **10:08** appears in item 10 on p. 9 for an FAA notification time the staff notes associate with military correspondence. The held FAA table AWA-922 p. 1 lines 22–23 separately prints **10:08*** for United 93 notification. A common displayed minute alone does not authenticate an email, the event, the clocks or a relationship between the files.
+
+The staff list records tests they considered in 2004. It is distinct from the interview passages on pp. 5–6. The source claims leave event definitions, channel coverage and the order of any informal relays unresolved. [Staff list, pp. 8–9](https://www.archives.gov/files/declassification/iscap/pdf/2011-048-doc39.pdf#page=8) · [OIG report, p. 4](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=4) · [FAA AWA-922](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7602360)
+
+## Verification and open records
+
+Preserved the complete official PDF and exact ZIP member, with SHA-256, access receipts, member CRC and physical page locators. Screened both nine-page files for text layers and visually inspected the identified handwriting. The FAA notes are image-only. The comparison checked the two displayed minute labels in held official sources. It did not decode audio, authenticate the possible log or verify the underlying correspondence. The first NARA body retrieval stopped at the configured 3 MB cap. The response declared 4,160,368 bytes. A separate bounded retrieval matched the declared length and ETag. [Source pins](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+- [ ] **PD07, liaison log and CMOC review.** Seek the original log, retention or disposition evidence, September 11 review agenda and attendance, chronology or minutes, and the particular notes or extracts furnished to the review. Test the **Col.** and **Capt.** references against the actual meeting records.
+- [ ] **PD01 / PD07, FAA custody.** Seek the FAA event-tracker forms, printouts, operator entries and filing or handoff list, separate from the operations-center participant log and phone-bridge recording lead. Recover the underlying military correspondence and native logs before testing the time labels.
+- [ ] **PD01–PD04.** The corrected FAA May 2003 answer, recipient acknowledgment, signed administrative decision and recommendation-level outcomes remain open. [OIG recommendations 1 and 2, p. 12](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=12)
